      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hoplocharax-goethei-4/",
      "html": "<p>Until recently Hoplocharax goethei was imported rather accidentally as cardinal tetra bycatch from Colombia and Brazil, mostly as single specimens. For some years a somewhat larger number of animals have been imported from Venezuela from time to time.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hoplocharax-goethei-4/",
      "html": "<p>In the aquarium it accepts any common fish food, including dry food. In nature it may prefer to eat fish larvae.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hoplocharax-goethei-4/",
      "html": "<p>The species grows to only 3-4 cm in length. At first glance it looks like perhaps the most common tetra in Amazonia, Hemigrammus bellottii, but it belongs to a completely different family and is actually a closer relative to predatory tetras such as the freshwater barracudas of the genus Acestrorhynchus. The dentition of Hoplocharax goethei is not made up of jagged incisors like Hemigrammus bellottii but of needle-sharp fangs. Nevertheless, this miniature predator is a peaceful animal that will not harm any of its equally sized tankmates. The species name goethei honours Charles Matthias Goethe (1875-1966), not Johann Wolfgang von Goethe. The bright red eye of Hoplocharax goethei is much more brilliantly coloured than in any other tetra species.</p>"
    }
  ],
